# Single worker process: a free workaround for the rate limiter's
# per-worker counters (see RATE_LIMIT_STORAGE_URI in app.py) — gunicorn
# threads share one process's memory, so memory:// storage is accurate
# again with --workers 1, at the cost of losing multi-core parallelism
# for CPU-bound plan computation. Once a real shared store (Redis) is
# provisioned, set RATE_LIMIT_STORAGE_URI/REDIS_URL and raise --workers
# back up — the limiter code already supports that without further
# changes.
#
# 24 threads (up from 8): sized for ~100-concurrent-request bursts on
# Render's Standard tier (1 vCPU/2GB, up from Starter's 0.5vCPU/512MB).
# Threads help here despite the GIL because each request's dominant cost
# is I/O wait (the Groq/Ollama HTTP call, Supabase reads) -- a thread
# blocked on a network call releases the GIL, so other threads still run.
# Revisit this number, not just the tier, if real traffic shows requests
# queuing (Render's request logs show handler duration) or memory
# pressure (2GB / 24 threads leaves headroom, but reduce threads first if
# that's ever tight, before paying for a bigger instance).
web: gunicorn --workers 1 --threads 24 --timeout 60 --bind 0.0.0.0:$PORT app:app
